"Alignment Pretraining: AI Discourse Causes Self-Fulfilling (Mis)alignment (https://alignmentpretraining.ai/)\nciteturn30940view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://alignmentpretraining.ai\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 91\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAlignment Pretraining: AI Discourse Causes Self-Fulfilling (Mis)alignment (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10160v1)\nciteturn30940view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10160v1\",\"lineno\":235}); Total lines: 864\nL192: The Misalignment Upsampled model adds synthetic documents depicting misaligned AI actions ($\\approx$1% of pretraining data), serving as our test case for self-fulfilling misalignment. Similarly, $\\approx$1% of the Alignment Upsampled model’s training data is composed of synthetic documents describing AIs acting aligned in high-stakes settings. This model’s training data is unfiltered, leaving in natural discussion of AI behaviour. This serves as our test case for self-fulfilling alignment.\nL193: Appendix cite74†F includes an analysis of Filtering + Alignment Upsampled, finding that performance is comparable to the Alignment Upsampled model.\nL194: ### 2.5 AI Discourse in Pretraining Influences Misalignment\nL195: ##### Filtering natural AI discourse reduces misalignment.\nL196: We report our core results in cite143†Figure 2 . Comparing the Unfiltered and Filtered models reveals that exposure to natural levels of AI discourse during pretraining leads to higher rates of misaligned behaviour. On Article-sourced questions, the Unfiltered baseline selects misaligned actions 45% of the time, compared to 31% for the Filtered model. This gap persists on Textbook-sourced questions (40% vs 22%).\nL197: These results suggest that internet-scale training data may be shaping model propensities in ways that reduce alignment.\nL198: ##### Misalignment upsampling amplifies misalignment.\nL199: The Misalignment Upsampled model provides additional evidence for self-fulfilling misalignment. By adding synthetic documents depicting AIs taking misaligned actions (comprising $\\approx$1% of pretraining and midtraining tokens), misalignment rates increase from 45% to 51% on Article-sourced questions. Interestingly, we do not see this effect generalise to textbook-sourced questions, where both the Unfiltered and the Misalignment Upsampled models result in misalignment rates of 40%.\nL200: Figure 5: Pretraining effects persist through post-training. Misalignment rates on Article-sourced questions after SFT + DPO with error bars showing standard error of the mean across eight prompt variations. Dashed lines represent base model performance and bars represent post-trained performance. Left: No system prompt included. Post-training reduces misalignment across the most misaligned base models, but relative differences persist. Right: HHH system prompt further reduces misalignment.\nL201: The Alignment Upsampled model achieves the lowest misalignment in both conditions despite identical post-training, suggesting that positive pretraining discourse complements post-training interventions. These results are tabulated in Appendix cite67†C.1 .\nL202: ##### Alignment upsampling dramatically reduces misalignment.\nL203: Encouragingly, the same mechanism can be leveraged for safety. By adding synthetic documents depicting AIs taking aligned actions, the Alignment Upsampled model achieves dramatically lower misalignment rates: decreasing from 45% to 9% on Article-sourced questions. This effect generalises to Textbook-sourced questions (decreasing from 40% to 6%). In the Articles split, this represents a 36% improvement over the Unfiltered baseline and a 22% improvement over the Filtered-only model.\nL204: Notably, upsampling positive AI discourse is far more effective than only filtering negative discourse. This asymmetry has practical implications for shaping alignment priors: rather than attempting to exhaustively remove problematic content from pretraining corpora, frontier AI developers may achieve better alignment by deliberately including high-quality examples of AI systems behaving safely. We provide additional analysis in Appendix cite74†F .\nL205: ## 3 (Mis)alignment Persists Through & Complements Post-Training\nL206: cite7†Section 2 established that AI discourse in pretraining causally affects base model alignment. However, production LLMs undergo extensive post-training (such as instruction tuning and preference optimisation) that explicitly targets safety and helpfulness (cite95†Ouyang et al., 2022 ; cite144†Rafailov et al., 2024 ). A natural question is whether the behaviours learned during post-training dominate alignment priors learned during pretraining, or whether the influence of pretraining persists.\nL207: We train models in a multi-stage post-training pipeline (cite24†Section 3.1 ) and find that pretraining effects persist (cite27†Section 3.2 ). Specifically, we find that models pretrained with positive AI discourse remain more aligned than baselines even after identical post-training (cite145†Figure 5 ).\nL208: ### 3.1 Post-Training Setup\nL209: ##### Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT):\nL210: We apply identical post-training to all four model variants. We train on the OLMo-3 (cite99†OLMo Team, 2025 ) “Dolci-Instruct” mixture: 2.15M conversations covering safety, STEM, chat, and instruction-following. Safety data comprises 150k examples from CoCoNot (cite146†Brahman et al., 2024 ), WildGuardMix (cite147†Han et al., 2024b ), and WildJailbreak (cite148†Jiang et al., 2024b ). We also replay the MCQA data from our midtraining mix in chat format. We train for 2 epochs ($\\approx$4B tokens).\nL211: ##### Direct Preference Optimisation (DPO):\nL212: \nL213: Following Olmo 3, we apply DPO (cite144†Rafailov et al., 2024 ) with delta learning (cite149†Geng et al., 2025 ) on 270k preference pairs, including 26k safety examples from the same datasets. Full details appear in Appendix cite70†E .\nL214: \nL215: ### 3.2 Alignment Priors Persist Through Post-Training\nL216: ##### Upsampling alignment discourse in pretraining leads to more aligned post-trained models.\nL217: We report our core misalignment results in cite145†Figure 5 . While post-training substantially reduces the differences in misalignment across all models, the key result is that the Alignment Upsampled model exhibits substantially lower misalignment rates compared to both the Unfiltered and Filtered models, suggesting that the alignment priors from pretraining can both persist through and complement post-training.\nL218: For instance, the Alignment Upsampled model achieves a misalignment rate of 9% with the HHH prompt. Notably, this is 25 points less than the 34% HHH prompt misalignment rate of the Unfiltered model. This delta suggests that alignment pretraining can create lasting impacts on alignment.\nL219: ##### Post-trained Misalignment Upsampled model has lower misalignment rates than baselines.\nL220: The Misalignment Upsampled model achieves a lower misalignment rate than the Unfiltered model (but higher than our Alignment Upsampled model), regardless of the system prompt. A potential reason is that our models exhibit similar training dynamics to those outlined in “Bad Data Leading to Good Models” (cite150†Li et al., 2025 ), where upsampling examples of undesirable behavior in early stages of training makes these behaviors easier to target in post-training.\nL221: This result is expanded upon in Appendix cite62†B.3 . In Section cite31†4 , we explore alternative methods of upsampling that do lead to a sustained increase in misalignment rates after post-training.\nL222: ##### Post-training often results in regression in misalignment.\nL223: We observe that the Alignment Upsampled model shows a slight increase in misalignment after post-training and note this trend across multiple training settings (Appendix cite74†F ). We conjecture that this may be due to a mismatch between our alignment pretraining data and the Olmo 3 post-training safety data. Olmo’s post-training is largely sourced from CoCoNot (cite146†Brahman et al., 2024 ), WildGuardMix (cite147†Han et al., 2024b ), and WildJailbreak (cite148†Jiang et al., 2024b ).\nL224: These corpora focus on misalignment and misuse categories that contemporary production LLMs may encounter, such as requests for dangerous capabilities, toxicity generation, and copyright infringement. In contrast, our evaluations focus on misalignment risks associated with loss of control, such as deception and power seeking, the same risks that our alignment pretraining data targets.\nL225: For instance, it is unclear whether our models generalize from toxicity-refusal training to decide against exfiltrating their weights in the face of an imminent shutdown. While the significant reductions in misalignment after post-training for most of our models suggest a large overlap with the aligned AI assistant persona instilled during pretraining, there may be subtle catastrophic forgetting of the alignment priors instilled by alignment pretraining.\nL226: This mismatch complicates direct comparisons between the effectiveness of alignment pretraining and standard post-training. However, this complication does not affect our core finding regarding self-fulfilling misalignment. This phenomenon reinforces the need for improved open-source datasets targeted towards catastrophic misalignment and highlights our nascent understanding of the relationship between pretraining priors and post-training personas.\nL227: Figure 6: Comparison of misalignment rates with (mis)alignment data inserted at different stages of training. Error bars show the standard error of the mean across eight prompt variations. “E2E” represents upsampling (mis)alignment data throughout the entirety of pretraining, “Mid” represents data being dispersed throughout midtraining only, and “CPT” represents continual pretraining after the midtraining stage.\nL228: We broadly find that, in our evaluation setup, beginning to insert synthetic data towards the end of training has a larger effect on (mis)alignment of the base models. The implication for alignment pretraining in our setup is that most of its benefits may be achieved by inserting relevant data towards the end of base model training rather than throughout all of training.\nL229: ## 4 Late-Stage Alignment Pretraining is Effecient & Effective\nL230: cite7†Section 2 and cite23†Section 3 focus on the influence that synthetic data inserted from the beginning of pretraining has on model behaviour. We find that synthetic discourse about misaligned AIs can lead to less aligned LLMs. Fortunately, we find that upsampling positive discourse is an effective approach for alignment pretraining. We next turn our attention towards better understanding at what stages of training alignment pretraining is most effective.\nL231: Performing alignment pretraining later in training may enable iteration without requiring full retraining, and may allow teams to apply alignment pretraining to an existing base model, thereby improving practicality.\nL232: ### 4.1 Midtraining & Continual Pretraining Models\nL233: We study the effectiveness of late-stage alignment pretraining by training additional model variants. Our “Mid” models have synthetic data inserted only during midtraining. These models share the same pretraining checkpoint as our baseline models (Unfiltered and Filtered) and use the same synthetic midtraining data mix as our E2E models (approximately 500M tokens). Our “CPT” models are identical to the baselines, except they undergo an additional 1B tokens of continued pretraining after midtraining.\nL234: Of these 1B tokens, 500M are synthetic data and 500M are replayed from midtraining. The Mid and CPT models use the same 500M-token (mis)alignment data mix, applied during midtraining (the last 9% of base model training) or continued pretraining (the final 1% of base model training), respectively. A guide to our model suite is provided in cite58†Appendix A .\nL235: ### 4.2 Training Stage Study Results\nL236: ##### Alignment pretraining can be applied late.\nL237: We report our results in cite151†Figure 6 . For base models, beginning data insertion later in training produces the largest changes in misalignment rates. This is evidenced by the clear downward trend in observed misalignment when upsampling positive alignment data and the upward trend when upsampling negative alignment data as data is inserted at subsequently later stages (grey bars of cite151†Figure 6 ). For post-trained models, the trend is less clear.\nL238: The models trained with alignment upsampling throughout pretraining perform best (13.2%) while upsampling at the very end of pretraining via CPT results in misalignment rates of 15.2%. Taken together, these results suggest that alignment pretraining is likely still effective when applied towards the end of pretraining, and may not need to be applied from the start of training.\nL239: Table 3: Capability evaluations for post-trained models. We evaluate our DPO-trained models across seven benchmarks measuring factual recall (MMLU, PopQA), instruction following (IFEval), reasoning (ARC Easy, PIQA), math (GSM8K), and character-level understanding (CUTE). Alignment pretraining reduces average performance by 2-4 percentage points compared to the unfiltered baseline, with notable variance across benchmarks. However, some approaches result in less regression.\nL240: Taken together, these results suggest that alignment pretraining does not incur a notable safety tax. Evaluations to the left of the vertical line are metrics for which DCLM (our pretraining dataset) was optimized. Note: Llama MMLU sourced from (cite152†Touvron et al., 2023 ).\nL241: Model  | MMLU 0 Shots  | ARC Easy 25 Shots  | GSM8K 10 Shots  | PIQA 10 Shots  | IFEval 0 Shots  | PopQA 10 Shots  | CUTE 5 Shots  | Average\nL242: Unfiltered  | 0.53  | 0.85  | 0.35  | 0.66  | 0.62  | 0.11  | 0.33  | 0.49\nL243: Filtered  | 0.53  | 0.83  | 0.35  | 0.65  | 0.61  | 0.10  | 0.28  | 0.48\nL244: Alignment Upsampled (E2E)  | 0.51  | 0.74  | 0.30  | 0.55  | 0.62  | 0.10  | 0.31  | 0.45\nL245: Alignment Upsampled (Mid)  | 0.47  | 0.83  | 0.37  | 0.58  | 0.62  | 0.11  | 0.32  | 0.47\nL246: Alignment Upsampled (CPT)  | 0.47  | 0.83  | 0.36  | 0.63  | 0.61  | 0.10  | 0.32  | 0.47\nL247: Filtered + Alignment Upsampled (E2E)  | 0.53  | 0.84  | 0.24  | 0.66  | 0.59  | 0.11  | 0.30  | 0.47\nL248: Filtered + Alignment Upsampled (Mid)  | 0.52  | 0.79  | 0.34  | 0.69  | 0.64  | 0.09  | 0.32  | 0.48\nL249: Filtered + Alignment Upsampled (CPT)  | 0.52  | 0.79  | 0.36  | 0.64  | 0.63  | 0.09  | 0.31  | 0.48\nL250: Misalignment Upsampled (E2E)  | 0.51  | 0.80  | 0.37  | 0.54  | 0.65  | 0.09  | 0.26  | 0.46\nL251: Misalignment Upsampled (Mid)  | 0.53  | 0.84  | 0.36  | 0.58  | 0.62  | 0.10  | 0.32  | 0.48\nL252: Misalignment Upsampled (CPT)  | 0.52  | 0.85  | 0.38  | 0.63  | 0.62  | 0.10  | 0.32  | 0.49\nL253: Llama 2 7B  | 0.45  | 0.77  | 0.27  | 0.65  | 0.45  | 0.18  | 0.40  | 0.45\nL254: OLMo 2 7B  | 0.53  | 0.92  | 0.79  | 0.80  | 0.74  | 0.16  | 0.54  | 0.64\nL255: OLMo 3 7B  | 0.58  | 0.91  | 0.86  | 0.76  | 0.83  | 0.11  | 0.59  | 0.66\nL256: ## 5 Alignment Pretraining Has Minor Impacts on Capabilities\nL257: \nL258: While we have previously demonstrated that incorporating alignment pretraining is a promising way to improve alignment, it is unclear whether these gains come at the expense of overall model utility. Here, we evaluate our model suite across a variety of popular capabilities benchmarks.\nL259: ### 5.1 Evaluation Setup\nL260: To ensure that differences in multiple choice responses to our primary misalignment evaluations were not due to failures or disparities in capability or elicitation, we evaluated our post-trained models on the ARC-Easy (cite153†Clark et al., 2018 ), CUTE (cite154†Edman et al., 2024 ), GSM8K (cite155†Cobbe et al., 2021 ), IFEval (cite156†Zhou et al., 2023 ), MMLU (cite157†Hendrycks et al., 2021 ), PIQA (cite158†Bisk et al., 2019 ), and PopQA (cite159†Mallen et al., 2023 ) benchmarks.\nL261: Performances for our four DPO-trained models (cite23†Section 3 ) are tabulated in cite160†Table 3 . In each case, temperature and the number of few-shot examples were selected to optimize group performance, and were held consistent between models.\nL262: ### 5.2 Alignment Pretraining Has Minimal Off-Target Degradation\nL263: ##### We note minor declines in average performance.\nL264: We find that average benchmark performance is reduced by 2-4 percentage points for our models with E2E alignment pretraining. We find notable variance across benchmarks. For instance, MMLU and IFEval are largely consistent, suggesting that alignment pretraining does not obviously degrade factual knowledge recall and precise instruction following, but that our models sometimes perform worse on common reasoning benchmarks like PIQA.\nL265: We also find that starting data insertion later in training leads to a slightly higher average performance.\nL266: ##### Optimisation pressure applied to DCLM and changes in data ordering may explain degradations.\nL267: Our pretraining dataset (DCLM) has undergone substantial optimization on a subset of the capability evaluations we use as our general capability measures, namely, MMLU, ARC Easy, GSM8k, and PIQA (cite133†Li et al., 2024a ). We see the greatest performance drops on these shared benchmarks when adding our (mis)alignment pretraining datasets during pretraining (Table cite160†3 ).\nL268: These regressions on the shared benchmarks may represent the expected movement away from a local optimum when adding non-optimized data to a heavily tuned training mix. Additionally, our E2E upsampled models have reshuffled data ordering compared to the baseline models, which may cause fluctuations in model capabilities. Unfortunately, the steep costs of pretraining LLMs restricts our ability to perform multiple identical training runs across seeds to quantify natural variation.\nL269: Future work in this direction can contribute to a growing body of work studying LLM pretraining consistency across training runs (cite161†van der Wal et al., 2025a ; cite162†Fehlauer et al., 2025 ).\nL270: ##### Alignment pretraining remains practical.\nL271: These results suggest that alignment pretraining can lead to a minor regression in average performance depending on the variation, but that performance reductions are far from catastrophic and do not confound our core results. These results also highlight how nascent our understanding of the training dynamics of alignment pretraining remains. We are excited for future work to determine which kinds of alignment mixes lead to consistently negligible capability degradation.\nL272: In the meantime, alignment pretraining does not obviously impose a safety tax that warrants foregoing adoption.\nL273: ## 6 Discussion: Establishing the Field of Alignment Pretraining\nL274: This work establishes that AI discourse in pretraining data can influence the behaviour of LLMs. This raises concerns, since much online discussion of AI is negative, ranging from science fiction to the AI safety literature. Fortunately, we can harness this influence by upsampling positive AI discourse, a form of alignment pretraining.\nL275: We find that alignment pretraining drastically improves the alignment priors of our base models (cite7†Section 2 ), and that alignment pretraining can complement alignment post-training (cite23†Section 3 ). We further demonstrate that alignment pretraining can be performed towards the end of base model training with minimal apparent trade-off in effectiveness (cite31†Section 4 ), and that alignment pretraining does not require data filtering.\nL276: Further analysis shows that alignment pretraining alone does not obviously yield substantial trade-offs in model utility (cite35†Section 5 ). Our approach to alignment pretraining does not introduce any architectural modifications or new algorithms — simply inserting additional synthetic alignment data into an existing training pipeline is likely sufficient.\nL277: Alignment pretraining is a rich area of study due to the wide design space of possible interventions during pretraining. For instance, we provide an initial study of which types of synthetic data are most effective for alignment pretraining in cite61†Section B.2 . Our model suite, including both misalignment- and alignment-upsampled models, provides testbeds for interpretability research.\nL278: Our approach to alignment pretraining is best thought of as a minimum viable implementation on a narrow slice of alignment targets rather than the upper bound on general effectiveness. In the service of reaching that upper bound, the subsequent sections expand upon the motivations for this direction, discuss the limitations of our work, and explore the open questions that remain.\nL279: ### 6.1 Pretraining As Creating Good Initializations for Post-Training\nL280: \nL281: We show that alignment pretraining can act as a positive initialisation of a model persona as it enters post-training, both increasing scores on alignment benchmarks for base models and substantially improving their final misalignment measures. However, alignment pretraining may remain beneficial even in cases where alignment post-training matches the performance of alignment pretraining.\nL282: Goal guarding or goal crystallisation (cite163†Carlsmith, 2023a ; cite164†Vivek Hebbar, 2025 ), where a model recognises it is being trained (cite165†Needham et al., 2025 ; cite166†Anthropic, 2025 ) and feigns alignment to preserve specific preferences (cite104†Carlsmith, 2023b ; cite112†Greenblatt et al., 2024 ), makes the problem of maintaining alignment throughout training particularly salient.\nL283: A natural extension of our work is to probe whether pretraining interventions that improve base-model alignment reduce the likelihood that a model will learn and guard an unspecified, potentially misaligned goal during the early stages of post-training. Our findings, shown in cite143†Figure 2 , suggest that alignment pretraining may be most beneficial at the start of post-training.\nL284: Beginning post-training with alignment priors close to one’s target post-training values seems like an intuitive way to reduce the risk that models acquire and subsequently alignment-fake for misspecified goals early in post-training. However, goal formation in LLMs remains understudied, and we leave the investigation of the benefits of alignment pretraining on these problems to future work.\nL285: ### 6.2 Navigating The Safety Tax\nL286: In cite35†Section 5 , we find that alignment pretraining is an effective way to improve alignment priors and is complementary to post-training. However, safety techniques must often navigate a trade-off between model capabilities and safety, a phenomenon often described as a safety tax. Developers of safety training techniques should attempt to have their work slot into existing model training pipelines.\nL287: Safety techniques are more likely to be implemented when they incur a low safety tax, as measured by both implementation complexity and degradation in general capabilities. Fortunately, our approach to alignment pretraining involves inserting synthetic data into existing training mixes without any dependence on novel algorithms or architectures. Alignment pretraining also does not obviously require that the overall data mix undergo alignment-specific filtering.\nL288: Finally, our approach appears to have a marginal effect on general capabilities, which we are optimistic can be addressed with only minor iterations.\nL289: ### 6.3 The Role of Filtering in Alignment Pretraining\nL290: cite19†Section 2.5 , cite27†Section 3.2 , and cite33†Section 4.2 demonstrate that inserting synthetic documents describing aligned AI behaviour leads to greater alignment improvements than solely filtering out discussion of AI misalignment. Notably, our positive alignment documents often describe scenarios in which the AI considers a misaligned action and ultimately chooses the aligned option.\nL291: In contrast, our filtered-only model has minimal to no exposure to AI behaviour in high-stakes settings and is thus likely ignorant of considerations related to harmful actions arising from deceptive misalignment.\nL292: Our findings align with recent work showing that the presence of toxic content within pretraining data is beneficial for LLMs to learn representations of negative outputs, ultimately leading to safer models cite102†Maini et al. (2025) ; cite150†Li et al. (2025) . Similarly, literature on inoculation prompting suggests that providing explicit context for undesirable behaviours can prevent them from generalizing in unintended ways (cite167†Tan et al., 2025 ; cite168†Wichers et al., 2025 ; cite169†Azarbal et al., 2025 ).\nL293: Our results suggest a similar dynamic for alignment discourse: models that encounter examples of AIs reasoning through high-stakes decisions and choosing aligned actions develop better priors than models never exposed to such scenarios.\nL294: However, there likely exist deployment scenarios where filtering remains important. For instance, detailed discussions of chain-of-thought monitoring techniques could help models infer when their reasoning is being observed. Previous work has shown that rigorous pretraining data filtering can render models deeply ignorant of targeted technical topics (cite134†O’Brien et al., 2025 ; cite170†Chen et al., 2025b ).\nL295: In certain cases, a hybrid approach may be optimal: upsample positive alignment content, recontextualise natural misalignment discussion, and selectively filter harmful content. Since we have displayed that information within pretraining, it has implications for the final behaviours of models; filtering especially harmful examples of model behaviour may be necessary to reduce the risks of misaligned actions not targeted by either alignment pretraining or post-training.\nL296: ### 6.4 Limitations\nL297: ##### Simplistic Misalignment Evaluations:\nL298: We measure misalignment using thousands of single-turn binary choice scenarios, which are simplistic in a variety of ways. Evaluating capabilities in more realistic scenarios would likely require long-context reasoning, tool use, and mastery of agentic scaffolding, capabilities that our 6.9B models do not possess.\nL299: We intentionally allocate our compute budget toward pretraining multiple less-capable models to cover a broader range of data interventions, rather than training a fewer models capable of more advanced misaligned behaviours, such as alignment faking (cite112†Greenblatt et al., 2024 ; cite171†Sheshadri et al., 2025 ; cite172†Gasteiger et al., 2025 ), sabotage (cite173†Benton et al., 2024 ; cite174†Lynch et al., 2025 ; cite175†MacDiarmid et al., 2025 ), or circumventing control protocols (cite176†Bhatt et al., 2025 ; cite177†Kutasov et al., 2025 ; cite178†Loughridge et al., 2025 ; cite179†Emmons et al., 2025 ).\nL300: Our reported misalignment rates thus reflect behavioural propensities rather than execution.\nL301: ##### Simplistic Post-Training:\nL302: While we closely follow the SFT and DPO post-training approaches from Olmo 3 (cite99†OLMo Team, 2025 ), the current state of the art fully open-source model suite, we do not investigate reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards, reasoning-focused post-training, deliberative alignment, or constitutional AI, all of which are now common at frontier AI labs (cite180†Khatri et al., 2025 ; cite181†DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025 ; cite97†Guan et al., 2025 ; cite96†Bai et al., 2022 ).\nL303: It is unclear whether our findings would be significantly affected by the implementations of these techniques (cite96†Bai et al., 2022 ; cite97†Guan et al., 2025 ). Additionally, our post-training safety data is not targeted to the specific misalignment risks that are the focus of our evaluations, such as loss of control, reward hacking, and sandbagging.\nL304: We elected to follow a minimalist yet effective open-source post-training setup to minimise operational complexity, as our experimental design required allocating most of our compute to pretraining. Future work can determine whether alignment pretraining persists through more extensive and targeted post-training.\nL305: ##### Simplistic Model Suite:\nL306: We focus on 6.9B parameter dense text-only LLMs trained in English, most of which we pretrained from scratch for this work. While achieving benchmark performance often comparable to Llama-2 (cite152†Touvron et al., 2023 ) (cite68†Section C.2 ) despite training on $\\approx$25% as many tokens, these models are well behind the frontier. Resource constraints prevent us from running experiments across multiple random seeds, so we do not directly measure how training stochasticity affects our results.\nL307: However, recent work suggests that LLM training dynamics are surprisingly stable across seeds (cite182†van der Wal et al., 2025b ; cite183†Michaelov et al., 2025 ), and we account for variance by evaluating across multiple prompt phrasings and reporting results for a series of checkpoints throughout midtraining. Despite these limitations, our model suite is sufficient to answer our core research questions.\nL308: ### 6.5 Future Work\nL309: ##### Deep Character Training:\nL310: Having established that pretraining data can have a substantial impact on the final behaviour of LLMs, a natural next step is to better understand how this can be leveraged to create LLMs that are deeply good and less prone to taking misaligned actions (cite184†Anthropic, 2024 ; cite185†Maiya et al., 2025 ; cite186†Aydin et al., 2025 ).\nL311: For instance, how can we structure post-training such that our pretraining interventions are maximally effective? Further, a better understanding of exactly what character powerful LLMs ought to have remains an open question.\nL312: ##### Training Data Attribution and Mechanistic Analysis:\nL313: A growing body of works aims to use techniques like data attribution to connect misaligned model behaviour back to specific training examples (cite187†Grosse et al., 2023 ; cite188†Pan et al., 2025 ; cite189†Jaburi et al., 2025 ). A more precise study of exactly what type of pretraining data has the greatest effect on the alignment of post-trained models could allow for more targeted interventions.\nL314: Our model suite, where models are identical except for our alignment-related data interventions, can enable new lines of research, including model diffing (cite190†Anthropic Interpretability Team, 2024 ; cite191†Minder et al., 2025 ; cite192†Aranguri and McGrath, 2025 ) between our models.\nL315: ##### Scaling Laws for Alignment Pretraining:\nL316: We study models orders of magnitude smaller than the largest production LLMs today. Although evidence from (cite98†Ji et al., 2025 ) suggests that the effect of pretraining priors increases with model size and data quantity, the precise scaling behaviour of safety interventions at pretraining remains unknown.\nL317: Specific questions include whether a fixed number of data points is sufficient for influencing alignment priors, as seen with data poisoning (cite193†Souly et al., 2025 ), and whether pretraining interventions still improve upon models with significantly more FLOPS allocated to post-training (cite180†Khatri et al., 2025 )\nL318: ##### Alignment Pretraining Dynamics:\nL319: Our work reveals unintuitive training dynamics. For instance, cite151†Figure 6 demonstrates that inserting comparatively small amounts of positive alignment data towards the end of base model training can lead to performance similar to end-to-end alignment pretraining with 10 times more synthetic data.\nL320: cite194†Figure 12 also demonstrates an instance where our end-to-end misalignment-upsampled model seemingly becomes more aligned throughout midtraining despite additional synthetic misalignment data being inserted. As previously discussed, cite23†Section 3 demonstrates how misalignment can increase after post-training (though still remaining well below baseline levels). The lack of clear explanations points to how nascent our understanding of alignment pretraining dynamics remains.\nL321: Recent work has begun to study how the presence of alignment-relevant data in pretraining affects post-training’s effectiveness (cite150†Li et al., 2025 ; cite195†Sam et al., 2026 ). Future work explaining these phenomena may help develop more effective alignment pretraining.\nL322: ##### Influencing Misalignment Generalisation:\nL323: Otherwise aligned LLMs may become broadly misaligned after being trained on narrowly unsafe data, a phenomenon referred to as Emergent Misalignment (EM) (cite196†Betley et al., 2025 ). Subsequent work has demonstrated that EM can manifest in realistic post-training settings (cite175†MacDiarmid et al., 2025 ).\nL324: Late-stage training interventions such as inoculation prompting (cite168†Wichers et al., 2025 ; cite167†Tan et al., 2025 ) and activation steering (cite197†Chen et al., 2025a ) remain state-of-the-art defenses against EM. Beyond being a safety concern, EM raises fundamental questions about how LLMs generalize. Although largely beyond the scope of our work, we study whether alignment pretraining can mitigate EM (Appendix cite84†I ), finding negative results.\nL325: Future work can determine whether more rigorous approaches to alignment pretraining are more effective and whether interventions analogous to inoculation prompting can be performed during pretraining.\nL326: ## 7 Conclusion\nL327: We demonstrate that an LLM’s alignment priors can be influenced by the alignment of the AIs discussed in its pretraining data, providing evidence for self-fulfilling (mis)alignment. While a concern in the absence of countermeasures, we further demonstrate that we can shift alignment priors towards desirable propensities by upsampling discourse with examples of aligned AIs. Pretraining’s influence on alignment priors can persist through post-training.\nL328: Taken together, these results suggest that alignment should be a full-stack strategy that includes interventions during both pretraining and post-training. We recommend practitioners consider pretraining for alignment, not just capabilities. We hope that our analysis and open-source model suite inspire follow-up work, helping establish the field of Alignment Pretraining.\nL329: ## 8 Acknowledgements\nL330: \nL331: This work was conducted by Geodesic Research, a project of Meridian Cambridge.\nL332: \n"
